# Marathon Mail OAuth client IDs — build-time bake-in.
#
# Copy this file to oauth-clients.env (gitignored) and fill in the
# IDs you registered per docs/MAIL_OAUTH_REGISTRATION.md. The
# build-marathon-mail-oauth-apk.sh wrapper sources it before running
# `cargo auditable build`, so `option_env!()` in src/main.rs picks the
# values up and bakes them as `&'static str` constants in the binary.
#
# These IDs are NOT credentials. PKCE installed-app OAuth replaces the
# client_secret with a per-flow proof of possession, so a published
# client_id alone cannot be used to impersonate Marathon. Thunderbird,
# Evolution, GNOME Online Accounts, Geary, and K-9 Mail all publish
# their client_ids in source for the same reason.
#
# Keeping the file out of git anyway is just hygiene: each release /
# fork can register its own IDs without colliding with upstream
# Marathon's quota, and the project's exact production IDs stay an
# implementation detail rather than something a fork inherits by
# default.
#
# Leave both unset (or empty) to build a community / fork apk that
# emits {"kind":"error","code":"oauth_not_configured",...} when the
# Sign-in-with-Google / Microsoft buttons are tapped — users fall
# back to the classic IMAP setup form for Fastmail / iCloud /
# Gmail-with-app-password / self-hosted, which works regardless.

# Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials →
# OAuth 2.0 Client IDs → Desktop app. Looks like
# 842XXXXXXX-XXXXXXXXXXX.apps.googleusercontent.com
MARATHON_DEFAULT_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""

# Microsoft Entra → App registrations → <your app> → Application
# (client) ID. UUID, e.g. 4f9d3e2a-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXXXXXXXXXX
MARATHON_DEFAULT_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=""
